/**
 * Site density — the third archetype.
 *
 * The constitution's control heights and 13–14px body are tuned for an
 * instrument: a surface an operator holds open all day, at a fixed distance,
 * where every extra pixel costs a row. A public site is the opposite reading
 * situation — a visitor arrives once, at arm's length, on an unknown display,
 * and clicks two or three things. The same numbers read as cramped there.
 *
 * So this layer keeps every role name and meaning and gives four of them the
 * site's scale. It is deliberately small: a difference that cannot be stated
 * as a reading situation is drift, not density.
 *
 * Import AFTER `constitution-theme.css`. A product that imports this declares
 * nothing itself — that is the point. Everything not listed here (radius, the
 * status ramp, motion, icon sizes, row padding, the rest of the type ramp) is
 * the constitution's, unchanged, because nothing about being a site argues for
 * a different answer.
 */

@theme inline {
  /* Body copy is read once, not scanned for hours; 13px is instrument density. */
  --text-body: 0.9375rem;

  /* A visitor's first click, not an operator's thousandth: 32 / 40 / 48px. */
  --spacing-control-compact: 2rem;
  --spacing-control: 2.5rem;
  --spacing-control-comfortable: 3rem;
}
